; queens.pol — the eight queens puzzle, in eight moves.
;
; The BOARD is not here. It lives in chess.lib.pol beside this file — sixty-four
; squares, their rows and diagonals, and the `free` test — because it is
; vocabulary rather than behaviour, and queens-unordered.pol says the same
; things about the same board. This file is only what may HAPPEN.
;
; THE CURSOR is what makes this eight moves instead of sixty-four. A move does
; not say WHICH queen it places — `cur` does, and the move advances it. So the
; moves index rows only, and column order is enforced by construction rather
; than by a conjunct in every guard.
;
; That needs both halves of §10.3/§10.1: `set` takes a CHAIN, so the cursor can
; walk itself with `(set cur.q cur.q.next)`, and effects are SIMULTANEOUS, so
; `(set cur.q.at S)` still writes the queen the cursor named when the move
; began. Write the two effects in either order and the space is identical.
;
; The ring closes it: after q8 the cursor lands on q1, which is already placed,
; so nothing is enabled — a finished board is a dead end, which is exactly what
; a finished board should be. All 92 of them are in `pol check`'s dead-end list.

(load "../libraries/chess.lib.pol")
(use board)
(initial empty)

(form (fill N S)
  => (transition N
       (when (and (not (defined cur.q.at)) (free p S)))
       (do (set cur.q.at S) (set cur.q cur.q.next))))

; The entire move set.
(fill place-1 cur.q.sq1)
(fill place-2 cur.q.sq2)
(fill place-3 cur.q.sq3)
(fill place-4 cur.q.sq4)
(fill place-5 cur.q.sq5)
(fill place-6 cur.q.sq6)
(fill place-7 cur.q.sq7)
(fill place-8 cur.q.sq8)
